## Chapter 4 Neutral Pion Measurements

Neutral pions were reconstructed from the photon candidate sample by means of their decay to two photons: \(\pi^{0}\to\gamma\gamma\). This channel is favorable for \(\pi^{0}\) reconstruction because of its large branching ratio \(\Gamma_{\pi^{0}\to\gamma\gamma}/\Gamma=98.8\%\). The mean lifetime of the \(\pi^{0}\) is of the order of \(10^{-16}\,\)s such that its decay vertex effectively coincides with the interaction vertex of the event. The invariant mass \(m_{\rm inv}\) of a pair of photons was calculated as

\[m_{\rm inv}=\sqrt{(p_{\gamma_{1}}+p_{\gamma_{2}})^{2}}=\sqrt{2E_{\gamma_{1}}E_ {\gamma_{2}}(1-\cos\psi)} \tag{4.1}\]

where \(p_{\gamma_{i}}\) denotes the four-momentum, \(E_{\gamma_{i}}\) the energy of the photon candidates, and \(\psi\) the opening angle between them. For two photons produced in a single \(\pi^{0}\) decay, the value of \(m_{\rm inv}\) is then equal to the \(\pi^{0}\) mass: \(m_{\pi^{0}}=135.0\,\)MeV.

### 4.1 Raw Yield Extraction

Due to the large multiplicity of produced particles in high energy nuclear collisions, many of the possible pairs did not consist of two photons coming from the same reaction \(\pi^{0}\to\gamma\gamma\). Hence, it was not possible to directly identify neutral pions. Instead, the \(\pi^{0}\) yield was extracted from the invariant mass distribution of all combinations of photon candidates, over many events. In this distribution the \(\pi^{0}\) signal emerged as a peak on top of a background largely formed by combinations of candidates which did not both originate from the same \(\pi^{0}\) decay. A thorough understanding of the behavior of the various components of this background turned out to be essential for a reliable extraction of the raw \(\pi^{0}\) yield.

#### Invariant Mass Spectra

A large part of the background in the invariant mass spectra was formed by combinations of uncorrelated photons. This background increased quadratically with the multiplicity of photon candidates in an event. However, the number of neutral pions was only proportional to that multiplicity and therefore the \(\pi^{0}\) signal relative to the underlying background improved when the multiplicity decreased.

This is confirmed by figure 4.1 which shows the spectra of \(m_{\rm inv}\) for consecutive \(p_{T}\) intervals of the photon pair. The \(\pi^{0}\) peak is clearly visible, spread around the theoretical value of the \(\pi^{0}\) mass as a result of the finite detector resolution. The figure illustrates that for increasing values of \(p_{T}\), where the multiplicity of photon candidates was lower, the peak-to-background ratio became larger, as expected.

In addition to the above, there was a component of the invariant mass background which was formed by correlated pairs of photons. Especially for neutral pions at high \(p_{T}\) it was important to consider the topology of the event. A high \(p_{T}\)\(\pi^{0}\) was likely to be part of a jet. The presence of this jet introduced angular correlations between pairs of photon candidates, even when they did not come from the same mesonic decay. Furthermore, the

