#### False Vetoes

The neutral pion yields were corrected for losses caused by accidental charged particle vetoes (CPV) applied to clusters from genuine photons. To determine the fraction of neutral pions which was lost as a result of these random rejections, the distance of any of the two photons to the closest track (\(D_{\rm track}\)) was determined separately for MB, HT1, and HT2 events. The obtained distribution of \(D_{\rm track}\), accumulated over all events, is shown in figure 4.18. The peak of this distribution near \(D_{\rm track}=0\) signals the presence of charged particles in the sample. The CPV criterion which was applied in our \(\pi^{0}\) analysis excluded all pairs with \(D_{\rm track}<5\,\)cm. However, in case only one of the two clusters had a charged track within the CPV radius, the other could still contribute in combination with the remaining clusters.

The background of random associations followed from a fit with the function

\[f_{\rm rnd}(x)=axe^{-bx} \tag{4.10}\]

in the range \(20<D_{\rm track}<50\,\)cm. This range was found to be well outside the \(D_{\rm track}\) distribution of simulated charged pions which indicates that the fit was applied to the random component of the distribution only. The resulting shape of \(f_{\rm rnd}\) is shown by the dashed curve in figure 4.18.

The calculation was performed for all \(p_{T}\) bins and all trigger classes separately. The area below the fit in the region \(0<D_{\rm track}<5\,\)cm was considered to be an estimate of the \(\pi^{0}\) losses as a result of false vetoes. The correction term \(\epsilon_{\rm CPV}\) was then defined as the ratio of the accepted pairs, i.e. those with \(D_{\rm track}\geq 5\,\)cm, and the total number of neutral clusters. The latter was determined as the integral of the \(D_{\rm track}\) distribution for \(D_{\rm track}\leq 20\,\)cm, summed with the area underneath the fit for \(D_{\rm track}<20\,\)cm. Equations 4.15 and 4.16 illustrate how the term \(\epsilon_{\rm CPV}\) was used to correct the raw \(\pi^{0}\) yield.

Figure 4.19 shows \(\epsilon_{\rm CPV}\) as a function of \(p_{T}\) for HT2 p+p events. The full line and the parameters correspond to a fit with the function

\[f_{\rm cpv}(x)=c+dx. \tag{4.11}\]

The value of \(f_{\rm cpv}\) can be interpreted as the fraction of true photon pairs which satisfied the CPV criterion: \(D_{\rm track}>5\,\)cm. The final parameters of \(f_{\rm cpv}\) are listed for completeness in table 4.3.

