Not all rejections of photon pairs from a genuine \(\pi^{0}\) were caused by random overlaps. In principle there was a possibility that one of those two photons converted early enough such that the electron and positron tracks were reconstructed in the TPC. In that case, the \(\pi^{0}\) could not be reconstructed. However, the correction of the data with \(\epsilon_{\rm reco}\) already accounted for losses due to conversions (see section 4.2.1) and therefore these were not considered here.

#### Correction for Finite Bin Width

The center of the \(p_{T}\) bins of the extracted \(\pi^{0}\) yield did not represent the mean \(p_{T}\) value in that bin. This was a consequence of the combination of a steeply falling \(p_{T}\) spectrum and the finite width of the bins. The choice was made to apply a correction to the measured yield, instead of shifting the \(p_{T}\) value of the point. The former was more convenient since eventually the ratios of different spectra had to be calculated.

The true invariant yield \(Y_{\rm true}\) at the center of a bin (\(p_{T}=p_{0}\)) was defined as

\[Y_{\rm true}=\frac{dN}{p_{T}dp_{T}}(p_{0}) \tag{4.12}\]

and the measured yield as

\[Y_{\rm meas}=\frac{N(p_{0})}{p_{0}\Delta} \tag{4.13}\]

where \(N(p_{0})\) represents the \(\pi^{0}\) yield after applying all previously described corrections and \(\Delta\) is the width of the bin at \(p_{0}\). This led to the following correction factor \(c_{\rm bin}\) in order to account for the overestimate of the yield as

\begin{table}
\begin{tabular}{l l c c c} \hline \hline system & par. & MB & HT1 & HT2 \\ \hline p+p & c & \(0.997\pm 0.002\) & \(1.001\pm 0.002\) & \(1.002\pm 0.003\) \\  & d & \(0.000\pm 0.001\) & \(-0.0017\pm 0.0004\) & \(-0.0018\pm 0.05\) \\ \hline d+Au & c & \(0.996\pm 0.001\) & \(0.996\pm 0.002\) & \(0.995\pm 0.002\) \\  & d & \(-0.0005\pm 0.0004\) & \(-0.0007\pm 0.0005\) & \(-0.0005\pm 0.0003\) \\ \end{tabular}
\end{table}
Table 4.3: The parameters of the fit to \(\epsilon_{\rm CPV}\) with the function \(f_{\rm cpv}\) defined in equation 4.11.

a result of the finite bin width:

\[c_{\rm bin}(p_{0}) = \frac{Y_{\rm true}}{Y_{\rm meas}}(p_{0}) \tag{4.14}\] \[= \frac{Y_{\rm true}(p_{0})}{\frac{1}{p_{0}\Delta}\int_{p_{0}-\Delta/ 2}^{p_{0}+\Delta/2}p_{T}\,Y_{\rm true}(p_{T})\,dp_{T}}.\]

The fit to the measured spectrum with \(f_{\rm yield}\) (see equation 4.9) was used to parametrize \(Y_{\rm true}\). The derived correction factor approaches unity for higher values of \(p_{T}\) where the yield falls off less rapidly. An exception was when the width of the bin increased, which enhanced the deviation from the true yield.

### 4.3 Results

The fully corrected Lorentz invariant differential \(\pi^{0}\) yield per minimum bias collision was derived as

\[E\,\frac{d^{3}N}{d^{3}p}=\frac{1}{2\pi p_{T}}\frac{d^{2}N}{dp_{T}dy}=\frac{1} {2\pi p_{T}}\frac{1}{N_{\rm ev}^{\rm MB}}\frac{1}{K(p_{T})}\frac{N_{\rm raw}^ {\pi^{0}}(p_{T})}{\Delta p_{T}\Delta y}, \tag{4.15}\]

where

\[K(p_{T})=\epsilon_{\rm reco}(p_{T})\,c_{\rm miss}^{-2}\,\epsilon_{\rm vert}\, \epsilon_{\rm CPV}\,c_{\rm bin}^{-1}\,\frac{\Gamma_{\gamma\gamma}}{\Gamma}, \tag{4.16}\]

and with the various symbols defined as

* \(N_{\rm raw}^{\pi^{0}}(p_{T})\) the uncorrected \(\pi^{0}\) yield per \(p_{T}\) bin
* \(N_{\rm ev}^{\rm MB}\) the number of accepted minimum bias events
* \(\epsilon_{\rm reco}(p_{T})\) the \(p_{T}\) dependent reconstruction efficiency
* \(\epsilon_{\rm vert}\) the vertex finding efficiency (d+Au only)
* \(c_{\rm miss}\) the single photon correction for conversions in the missing material
* \(\epsilon_{\rm CPV}\) the correction for false vetoes of photon pairs
* \(c_{\rm bin}\) the correction for finite bin width
* \(\Gamma_{\gamma\gamma}/\Gamma\) the branching ratio of the process \(\pi^{0}\to\gamma\gamma\)
* \(\Delta p_{T},\Delta y\) the width of the \(p_{T}\) and \(y\) bin, respectively In case of hightower triggered events, the above equations were modified to account for the pre-scaling of the minimum bias and HT1 event rates. Therefore, \(N_{\rm ev}^{\rm MB}\) was replaced by the equivalent number of minimum bias events which would have resulted in the HT2 data sample. The latter was equal to the sum over all events of the minimum bias pre-scales \({\rm ps}_{i}^{\rm MB}\) and given by

\[N_{\rm ev}^{\rm HT2}=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\rm ev}^{\rm MB}}{\rm ps}_{i}^{\rm MB}. \tag{4.17}\]

Since the HT1 event rate was pre-scaled as well, the expression for \(N_{\rm ev}^{\rm HT1}\) which was used is

\[N_{\rm ev}^{\rm HT1}=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\rm ev}^{\rm MB}}{\rm ps}_{i}^{\rm MB}/{\rm ps }_{\rm eff}^{\rm HT1} \tag{4.18}\]

with

\[{\rm ps}_{\rm eff}^{\rm HT1}=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\rm ev}^{\rm HT1}}{\rm ps}_{i}^{ \rm HT1}/N_{\rm ev}^{\rm HT1}. \tag{4.19}\]

The available statistics were not sufficient to determine \(N_{\rm raw}^{\pi^{0}}\) differentially in \(p_{T}\) and \(y\) simultaneously. Therefore, it was assumed that near mid-rapidity the production of neutral pions was independent of \(y\), just like the input distribution of the Monte-Carlo analysis used to determine \(\epsilon_{\rm reco}(p_{T})\). A validation of this assumption followed from the consistency of the raw \(\pi^{0}\) yield from experiment and simulation for different values of \(y\) and integrated over \(p_{T}\), as shown by figure 4.20. The figure also illustrates the selected range of rapidity \(y\) of neutral pions, \(0.1<y<0.9\), which was used in case of p+p as well as d+Au collisions.

The second implicit assumption in equation 4.15 was that the contribution to the \(\pi^{0}\) yield from events without a reconstructed vertex was negligible. This was confirmed with a HIJING [83] simulation of d+Au collisions within the GEANT framework. The per-event \(\pi^{0}\) yield in events without a primary vertex was indeed more than an order of magnitude lower, as illustrated by figure 4.21. Hence, the possible effect on the value of the invariant yield was found to be smaller than 0.2% for \(p_{T}>1\,{\rm GeV}/c\).

#### Calculation of the Differential Cross Section

For a collision of two protons, the total cross section \(\sigma_{\rm tot}\) can be divided into an elastic and an inelastic cross section:

\[\sigma_{\rm tot}=\sigma_{\rm el}+\sigma_{\rm inel} \tag{4.20}\]Figure 4.21: The \(\pi^{0}\) yield per event from HIJING events with and without a reconstructed primary vertex.

Figure 4.20: A comparison of \(dN_{\rm raw}^{\pi^{0}}/dy\) from p+p data and from the Monte-Carlo simulation.

The inelastic part consists of a single-diffractive (SD), double-diffractive (DD), and non-diffractive cross section (ND). The sum of the DD and ND cross sections is defined as the non-single-diffractive cross section (NSD).

Starting from the invariant yield, it follows that

\[\frac{dN}{dp_{T}} = \frac{1}{\sigma_{\rm BBC}}\,\frac{d\sigma_{\rm BBC}}{dp_{T}} \tag{4.21}\] \[= \frac{1}{\sigma_{\rm BBC}}\,\frac{d\sigma_{\rm inel}}{dp_{T}} \times\left(\frac{d\sigma_{\rm NSD}}{dp_{T}}\bigg{/}\frac{d\sigma_{\rm inel}}{ dp_{T}}\right)\times\left(\frac{d\sigma_{\rm BBC}}{dp_{T}}\bigg{/}\frac{d\sigma_{\rm NSD }}{dp_{T}}\right)\] \[= \frac{\epsilon_{\rm BBC}}{\sigma_{\rm BBC}}\,\frac{d\sigma_{\rm inel }}{dp_{T}}\times\left(\frac{d\sigma_{\rm NSD}}{dp_{T}}\bigg{/}\frac{d\sigma_{ \rm inel}}{dp_{T}}\right)\]

where \(d\sigma_{\rm BBC}/dp_{T}\) is the differential cross section for BBC triggered collisions. In general, \(d\sigma_{\chi}/dp_{T}\) was defined as the differential cross section for the process \({\rm p+p\rightarrow\pi^{0}+X}\) for interactions of type \(\chi\). Additionally, we have used in the last step above that the BBC efficiency was defined as

\[\epsilon_{\rm BBC}=\left(\frac{d\sigma_{\rm BBC}}{dp_{T}}\bigg{/}\frac{d\sigma _{\rm NSD}}{dp_{T}}\right), \tag{4.22}\]

which corresponds to the fraction of \(d\sigma_{\rm NSD}/dp_{T}\) satisfying the minimum bias condition as described in section 2.3.2. In what follows, we will demonstrate that the ratio of \(d\sigma_{\rm NSD}/dp_{T}\) and \(d\sigma_{\rm inel}/dp_{T}\) approximately equals unity.

Figure 4.22: The charged particle yields versus \(\eta\) measured by the UA5 collaboration. The left (right) panel shows the results in case of inelastic (NSD) interactions (figure from [51]).

The UA5 collaboration have measured the charged particle multiplicity in \(\mathrm{p}+\bar{\mathrm{p}}\) collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=200\,\mathrm{GeV}\) as a function of pseudo-rapidity [87]. The experimental results are shown in figure 4.22 in case of inelastic collisions (left panel) and NSD collisions (right panel). From these data we estimated that

\[\left(\frac{d\sigma_{\mathrm{inel}}}{d\eta}\bigg{/}\frac{d\sigma_{\mathrm{NSD }}}{d\eta}\right)\approx(0.9\pm 0.1)\times\frac{\sigma_{\mathrm{inel}}}{ \sigma_{\mathrm{NSD}}} \tag{4.23}\]

in the range \(0.0<\eta<1.0\). The same collaboration measured that \(\sigma_{\mathrm{SD}}=4.8\pm 0.5\pm 0.8\,\mathrm{mb}\) and \(\sigma_{\mathrm{inel}}=41.8\pm 0.6\,\mathrm{mb}\)[88], which implies that

\[\left(\frac{d\sigma_{\mathrm{inel}}}{d\eta}\bigg{/}\frac{d\sigma_{\mathrm{NSD }}}{d\eta}\right)\approx 1.02\pm 0.2. \tag{4.24}\]

In practice, the contribution from SD collisions falls off more rapidly with increasing \(p_{T}\) and results from a Pythia simulation have demonstrated that the ratio in equation 4.24 effectively equals unity for \(p_{T}\) values greater than \(1.2\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\)[71]. After substituting this result in equation 4.21, the differential cross section of inclusive \(\pi^{0}\) production in inelastic p+p collisions follows as

\[E\frac{d^{3}\sigma_{\mathrm{inel}}}{d^{3}p}=\frac{\sigma_{\mathrm{BBC}}}{ \epsilon_{\mathrm{BBC}}}\frac{1}{2\pi p_{T}}\frac{d^{2}N}{dp_{T}dy}. \tag{4.25}\]

This is the expression which we have used to convert our Lorentz invariant yields into cross sections.

