To exploit the fact that the inclusive photon and decay photon yields have many positively correlated uncertainties, we studied the direct photon yield in terms of the double ratio \(R_{\gamma}\):

\[R_{\gamma}=\frac{\gamma_{\rm incl}/\pi^{0}}{\gamma_{\rm decay}/\pi^{0}} \tag{5.2}\]

where the numerator equals the point-to-point ratio of the measured spectra of inclusive photons and neutral pions. The denominator is the simulated background contribution from decay processes divided by the parametrized \(\pi^{0}\) yield. It follows that

\[R_{\gamma}=1+\frac{\gamma_{\rm direct}}{\gamma_{\rm decay}} \tag{5.3}\]

which therefore serves as an indicator of a direct photon signal (\(R_{\gamma}>1\)).

Figure 5.1: The flow of the direct photon analysis.

The absolute direct photon yields can subsequently be determined as

\[\gamma_{\rm direct}=\left(1-R_{\gamma}^{-1}\right)\times\gamma_{\rm incl} \tag{5.4}\]

where the systematic uncertainties which canceled in the double ratio will have to be included again.

### 5.1 Inclusive Photons

The reconstruction of the \(\gamma_{\rm incl}\) spectrum was in many ways similar to that of the neutral pions described in the previous chapter. The uncorrected \(\gamma_{\rm incl}\) spectrum was extracted from the same data sample, using identical event cuts and the same sample of photon candidates. However, there were no rigorous constraints on the purity of the photon candidates used for the \(\pi^{0}\) invariant mass analysis. Remaining contributions from charged particles and neutral hadrons could be identified afterwards, in terms of the combinatorial background in the mass distributions. In contrast to the latter, the uncorrected inclusive photon sample \(N_{\rm incl}^{\rm raw}\) was obtained from an explicit subtraction of such backgrounds:

\[N_{\rm incl}^{\rm raw}=\left(1-C_{0}\right)\left(1-C_{\pm}\right)N_{\rm cand} \tag{5.5}\]

where the correction terms \(C_{0}\) and \(C_{\pm}\) represent the fractional contamination by neutral hadrons and charged particles, respectively, to the yield of photon candidates \(N_{\rm cand}\).

The Lorentz-invariant yield of inclusive photons was defined, analogously to that of neutral pions in equations 4.15 and 4.16, as

\[\frac{1}{2\pi p_{T}}\frac{d^{2}N}{dp_{T}dy}(p_{T})=\frac{1}{2\pi p_{T}}\frac{1 }{N_{\rm ev}^{\rm MB}}\frac{1}{K(p_{T})}\frac{N_{\rm incl}^{\rm raw}(p_{T})}{ \Delta p_{T}\Delta y}, \tag{5.6}\]

The correction term in the denominator is given by

\[K(p_{T})=\epsilon_{\rm reco}^{\gamma}(p_{T})\,c_{\rm miss}^{-1}\,\epsilon_{\rm vert }\,\epsilon_{\rm CPV}^{\gamma}\,c_{\rm bin}^{-1}. \tag{5.7}\]

The two factors in the expression for \(K(p_{T})\) which did not already appear in the formula for the \(\pi^{0}\) invariant yield are

* \(\epsilon_{\rm reco}^{\gamma}(p_{T})\) the single photon reconstruction efficiency
* \(\epsilon_{\rm CPV}^{\gamma}\) the correction for false vetoes in case of single photon candidatesThe inverse correction factor \(\epsilon_{\rm reco}^{\gamma}\) contained the convoluted effects on the inclusive photon yield from the limited acceptance, the detection and reconstruction efficiency, as well as energy smearing1. However, as will be explained in section 5.1.3 below, \(\epsilon_{\rm reco}^{\gamma}\) contained two quantitatively different components which were used to separately correct the raw photon yield from \(\pi^{0}\) decays and from the complementary sources.

Footnote 1: Hence, the inverse correction factor \(\epsilon_{\rm reco}^{\gamma}\) is in principle not an efficiency, as was already discussed in section 4.2.

#### Neutral Hadron Contamination

The term \(C_{0}\) in equation 5.5 was defined as the number of reconstructed showers generated by neutral hadrons relative to the total number of showers in the photon candidate sample. The STAR detector has no means of directly identifying neutrons (n) and antineutrons (\(\bar{\rm n}\)). Therefore the contamination had to be simulated and subtracted using the measured (anti)proton spectra to estimate its size.

Internal STAR measurements of the p and \(\bar{\rm p}\) yields in d+Au (2003) and p+p (2001) collisions were reported in [90]. These yields were parametrized as

\[\frac{1}{2\pi p_{T}}\frac{d^{2}N}{dp_{T}dy}=B\,\left(1+\frac{m_{T}-m_{0}}{nT} \right)^{-n} \tag{5.8}\]

with the parameters listed in table 5.1 and \(m_{0}\) equal to the proton mass. We assumed that their primary production rates were equal to those of the n and \(\bar{\rm n}\) yields, respectively. However, the contribution from \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) was not subtracted in case of the STAR p and \(\bar{\rm p}\) measurements.

The ratio of hyperon and proton spectra, \(\Lambda/{\rm p}\equiv\delta_{\Lambda}\), was estimated to be approximately 20% [90]. The hyperons contribute to the proton yield through the decay \(\Lambda\to{\rm p}+\pi^{-}\). The latter has a branching ratio equal to 63.9% which is different from the decay \(\Lambda\to{\rm n}+\pi^{0}\) (35.8%). Hence, the estimated yield of neutrons was determined from the measured proton yield as

\[{\rm n}=(1-\delta_{\Lambda})\,{\rm p}_{\rm prod}+\delta_{\Lambda}\frac{\Gamma( \Lambda\to{\rm n}\pi^{0})}{\Gamma(\Lambda\to{\rm p}\pi^{-})}\,{\rm p}_{\rm prod }+\frac{A-Z}{Z}{\rm p}_{\rm part} \tag{5.9}\]

The last term on the right is the scaled yield of participant protons (\({\rm p}_{\rm part}\)) to account for the different number of neutrons versus protons in the gold core (\(A=197\), \(Z=79\)). The yield of protons produced in the actual collision is written as \({\rm p}_{\rm prod}\). The sum of those two was, by definition, equal to the measured inclusive proton yield. Finally, the produced proton spectrum \({\rm p}_{\rm prod}\) was assumed to match that of the antiprotons since the latter does not contain participant contributions. The expression for the antineutron yield is similar to equation 5.9 without the participant term \(\mathrm{p_{part}}\).

The largest neutral contamination was caused by antineutrons. The annihilation process, e.g. \(\bar{\mathrm{n}}+p\to 2\pi^{+}\pi^{-}\pi^{0}\), in the lead of the calorimeter could initiate an electromagnetic shower which did not necessarily develop in the same direction as the incident antineutron. Moreover, the available energy for the reaction products included the energy of the nucleon at rest (\(\sim 1\,\mathrm{GeV}\)). The measured energy of especially the slow antineutrons could therefore be significantly increased as compared to the detection of photons with similar momenta, the potential shift being as large as approximately twice the rest mass of a nucleon.

To study the contamination of our photon candidate spectrum, approximately \(3\times 10^{6}\) antineutrons were generated and processed through the GEANT simulation with the following settings:

* \(0<p_{T}<10\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\)
* \(-0.3<y<1.2\)
* \(0<\phi<2\pi\)
* \(dN/dy=dN/d\phi=\mathrm{constant}\), \(dN/dp_{T}\sim\exp(-p_{T}/(2\,\mathrm{GeV}/c))\)

The non-uniform \(p_{T}\) distribution was chosen to provide sufficient statistics at low values of \(p_{T}\) where the slow antineutrons constituted a significant source of contamination. The GEANT framework was initialized with the FLUKA program [89] to describe the particle transport and the interactions in the detector material. Note that the parametrizations of the \(\mathrm{p}\) and \(\bar{\mathrm{p}}\) yields were not only used to assign a weight to the Monte-Carlo events but also to determine the absolute contamination of the photon sample. The latter was divided by the number of photon candidates from experiment which resulted in \(C_{0}\).

